================================================================================
AUTHORING RULES - 4. When to use.md
================================================================================
Purpose: say WHICH NEEDS `<{{componentName}}>` answers, and the near needs it
         does not answer, each with what answers it instead. The reader is an
         AI agent picking a component: `find_component` of the MCP server
         returns the `Not for` lines beside each candidate (epic #1579).

Companion docs to read first (do not re-derive their content here):
  - projects/stencil/SPEC.md - "Per-component usage docs", "`4. When to use.md`
                               and `5. Placement context.md`": the format, and
                               what `npm run check.usage-docs` verifies in CI.
  - docs/agents/patterns/    - the patterns a `Not for` line may name by id.

MUST contain (in this exact order, and nothing else):
  1. "#### Use it for": one list item per need, on one line, written as the
     need ("The person must ..."), with the criterion that tells this
     component from its neighbours inside the line: replaces the content or
     narrows the same list, steps in a fixed order and how many, can be
     undone or not, the kind of view (a task done once, a tool used over
     time), the container (a page, a dialog).
  2. "#### Not for": one list item per near need it does not answer. The
     need, a colon (the FIRST ": " of the line), then what answers it in
     inline code: a component (`mds-tab`), a pattern by its id
     (`confirm-action`), or `gap` when Magma has none. A line may name more
     than one answer and add a criterion after it; other inline code in the
     line (`href`, `type="radio"`) is not an answer.

CHECKED IN CI (npm run check.usage-docs):
  - the two headings, in this order, and only list items under them;
  - every `Not for` line has its colon and at least one answer that exists;
  - a `Not for` that names a component with its own `4. When to use.md` finds
    its need among that component's `Use it for` lines (shared words).

MUST NOT contain:
  - What the component is and how it behaves (that is `1. Description.md`).
  - Where it goes in a view (that is `5. Placement context.md`).
  - Code blocks, paragraphs, nested or wrapped items.

STYLE & LENGTH:
  - Plain, short sentences; 2-6 `Use it for` lines, as many `Not for` lines
    as there are near needs an agent may confuse with this one.
  - Delete this comment block before committing.
